- * @details As shipped, friction takes the normal force of the start of the step, so at an impact it arrives
- * one step late. The shipped switch implicitNormalForceForDissipation moves friction AND contact damping to
- * the force of the step being solved, and the damping term's derivative is then incomplete. With this on,
- * Coulomb friction alone takes the whole normal force of the step being solved (the penalty force at the
- * current distance plus the damping part as it is applied to the body, never below zero). Contact damping
- * and viscous friction keep the start-of-step force, so their derivatives stay exact. The derivative of the
- * step's normal force is left out of friction's own derivative, as SuperDex leaves it out for its implicit
- * setting, and friction's Hessian is then SuperDex's fitted one: the exact one is not positive definite once
- * the normal force moves within the step, and the solver diverged on it.
